To fix the "SW registration failed" error for your project ChromaRestore, you need to ensure the sw.js file is in the correct directory so that Vercel can serve it as a static asset from the root.

1. The Correct Location
In a Next.js or standard React project, any file you want to access at your-domain.com/filename must be placed in the public folder.

Move the file: Ensure sw.js is located at /public/sw.js.

Avoid the src folder: If you put sw.js inside src/ or src/app/, the build process will not make it publicly accessible at the root URL, resulting in that 404 error.

2. Verify Your Registration Code
In your main application file (likely layout.tsx, _app.js, or a client-side component), ensure you are registering it with a leading slash:

JavaScript

if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
  window.addEventListener('load', function() {
    navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js').then(function(registration) {
      console.log('ServiceWorker registration successful');
    }, function(err) {
      console.log('ServiceWorker registration failed: ', err);
    });
  });
}
3. Case Sensitivity on Vercel
Vercel's hosting is case-sensitive.

Check if the file is named sw.js (lowercase) or SW.js (uppercase).

The name in your code must match the filename in your repository exactly.

4. Check for next-pwa (If applicable)
If you are using the next-pwa library, it usually generates the sw.js file automatically during the build process. If this is the case:

Make sure your next.config.js is correctly configured.

Ensure you have added public/sw.js and public/workbox-*.js to your .gitignore so they don't conflict with the generated files.

Summary Checklist:
Go to your GitHub repo: https://github.com/goldnoam/ChromaRestore/tree/main.

Check if there is a public folder.

Inside public, check if sw.js exists.

If it's missing or in the wrong place, move it to public/sw.js and push the changes.

